course: everyday
lesson: 64
title: Birds, reptiles and choosing a pet
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A pet is not a toy for a birthday: it lives for years and needs food, cleaning and company every single day, so a family thinks hard and learns about the animal before getting one.
explain: Birds are flock animals that need friends, space to fly and long nights of sleep, and their small bodies are very sensitive: fumes from an overheated non-stick pan, avocado and chocolate can all kill a pet bird.
explain: Reptiles such as lizards, snakes and tortoises cannot make their own body heat, so they rely on the lamp and heat mat an adult sets up; they also often carry salmonella germs even when they look healthy.
explain: Many foods we enjoy are poisonous to pets, including onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes, salt and the sweetener xylitol, so pets eat their own food, not our leftovers.
explain: A boy in Arizona wants a bearded dragon for his birthday. First his family talks about who will feed it, keep its lamp on and clean it every day for many years, and they learn what it needs before they choose one from a good place.
explain: A family in California got a pet turtle; their little boy kissed it and later became ill with Salmonella, a germ many reptiles carry.
explain: A girl in Australia's parents bought a parrot without knowing it could live for decades and scream for hours when bored; within a year they had to find it a new home.
fact: Budgies are flock birds that need a friend, a big cage and time to fly in a safe room.
fact: Before a bird flies indoors you close windows and doors, cover mirrors and turn off fans.
fact: Fumes from overheated non-stick pans can kill birds quickly, so birds are kept out of the kitchen.
fact: Avocado and chocolate are poisonous to pet birds.
fact: Birds need about ten to twelve hours of sleep, so covering part of the cage at night helps.
fact: Reptiles often carry salmonella germs, so you wash your hands with soap after every touch.
fact: Reptiles cannot make their own body heat and get ill if they are kept too cold.
fact: A tortoise should hibernate only as a vet advises, because wrong hibernation can make it very ill.
fact: Many lizards can drop their tail to escape, so you never hold a lizard by its tail.
fact: A pet lives for years and needs care every day, so a family agrees who will do each job before getting one.
fact: Onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes, salt and xylitol sweetener can make pets very ill.
fact: Pets should not lick your mouth or face, because animals' mouths carry germs.
fact: Any animal can carry germs, even one that looks clean and healthy, so you wash your hands after touching pets.
fact: A pet is best got from a rescue centre or a good breeder, after learning about it; animals sold cheaply by strangers, from vans or markets, are often ill.
fact: Pets cannot be left alone for days, so a trusted person or pet sitter looks after them during holidays.
fact: An overweight pet gets ill more often and lives a shorter life, so it needs fewer treats and more play.
fact: Reptiles such as turtles, snakes and lizards often carry Salmonella germs that can make people sick.
fact: Some parrots can live for more than 50 years.
exercise: Why does a budgie need a friend and a big cage? | Budgies are flock birds and get lonely and bored | Budgies get far too cold when they live on their own | One budgie cannot eat alone | A big cage keeps cats away
exercise: What must you do before letting a pet bird fly around the house? | Close windows and doors, cover mirrors and turn off fans | Open a window so it gets fresh air | Turn on the ceiling fan to keep it cool while it flies about | Put a cat in the room to watch it
exercise: Why should a pet bird be kept out of the kitchen? | Fumes from overheated non-stick pans can kill birds | Birds do not like the smell of cooking food at all | Kitchens are too cold for birds | Birds might eat all the bread
exercise: Your parrot wants a bite of your avocado toast. What should you do? | Never give it avocado, it is poisonous to birds | Give it a small bite as a treat | Give it the toast but not the avocado butter | Mash the avocado so it is easier to eat
exercise: Is it all right to cover part of a bird cage at night? | Yes, it helps the bird sleep in the dark and quiet | No, birds must be able to see the light all through the night | No, birds never sleep | Yes, but only in the daytime
exercise: What must you do after holding a pet lizard or tortoise? | Wash your hands with soap | Wipe them on your trousers | Nothing, if the animal looks healthy | Blow on them until they're dry
exercise: Why does a bearded dragon need its heat lamp kept on? | Reptiles cannot make their own body heat | The lamp keeps flies away | It needs light to find its food | The lamp stops it from growing too big for its tank
exercise: Winter is coming and your tortoise is slowing down. What should your family do about hibernation? | Follow a vet's advice | Put it in the freezer to sleep | Leave it in the garden under a leaf | Wake it up every day to feed it
exercise: Why should you never pick up a lizard by its tail? | It may drop its tail to escape, which is stressful | The tail is where it keeps all of its stored food and water | It will turn a different colour | Its tail is poisonous to touch
exercise: You ask for a puppy for your birthday. What should your family talk about first? | Who will feed, clean and walk it every day for its whole life | What colour collar it should wear | Which friends will want to come round and see it | How many photos to take of it
exercise: Where is a good place to get a pet? | A rescue centre or a good breeder, after learning about the animal | Whoever sells it the cheapest online | A stranger at the market with a box | Any place that has one ready today, so you don't have to wait for it
exercise: Your dog begs for a grape from your plate. What should you do? | Not give it, grapes can make dogs very ill | Give it just one as a treat | Give it the grape without the skin, which is the only bad part | Give it a raisin instead
exercise: Your dog tries to lick your mouth. What is best? | Do not let it lick your mouth or face | Let it, dogs' mouths are cleaner than ours | Let it, but only after it has had dinner | Lick it back to show it how much you love it
exercise: Your family is going on holiday for a week. What about the cat? | Ask a trusted person or pet sitter to look after it | Leave lots of food and go | Shut it in the garage with plenty of water and food | Let it out to look after itself
exercise: Your cat is getting fat. What should you do? | Give fewer treats and play with it more | Give it more food so it is happy and full | Give it a bowl of milk instead of its food | Keep it in a small cage so it rests and stops eating so much
test: A girl in Melbourne lets her budgie fly in the lounge while the ceiling fan is spinning. Why is that dangerous? | The bird can fly into the fan and be badly hurt | The fan makes the bird too cold to fly | Budgies are frightened of the colour of fans | The fan will blow away its food
test: A boy in Florida holds his pet turtle and then eats a sandwich without washing his hands. What could happen? | He could catch salmonella germs from the turtle | Nothing, because the turtle looked clean and healthy | The turtle could catch his cold | His sandwich would taste of the turtle
test: A family in Saskatchewan keeps their lizard in a cold room and turns its lamp off to save power. What will probably happen? | The lizard may get ill because it cannot warm itself | The lizard will grow fur to keep warm | Nothing, lizards like the cold | The lizard will make its own heat by running round and round its tank
test: At a barbecue, a guest gives a dog leftovers with onions and grapes. Why should the children stop this? | Onions and grapes can make dogs very ill | Dogs never eat food from barbecues | It will make the dog much too thirsty on a hot day | Dogs prefer sweets to onions
test: A bird sits under a cover in a quiet room from evening till morning. Why is this good for it? | Birds need about ten to twelve hours of dark, quiet sleep | Birds are afraid of seeing the moon | It stops the bird from eating at night and getting fat | It keeps the bird warm like a coat
test: A girl wants a dog but her family is away from home all day and travels often. What is the wise thing to think about first? | Who will care for the dog every day and when they travel | Which breed looks the best in photos | How quickly they can get a dog this week | Whether the dog can be left alone for days
test: A man sells puppies cheaply from the back of his van. Why is this a risky place to buy a pet? | Pets from bad sellers are often ill | Puppies from vans are always too big | Vans are where rescue centres keep pets | Cheap puppies never grow up properly
test: A boy picks up his gecko by the tail and the tail drops off. What does this show? | Many lizards drop their tail to escape when grabbed | The gecko was already very ill | Geckos' tails are made of rubber and come off easily | The boy pulled too gently
test: A dog has become fat from lots of treats. Which plan helps it most? | Fewer treats and more walks and play | Double treats so it is not sad about being fat | Sleep more to save energy | Eat only cake on Sundays
test: letting the budgie out for a fly, anything i gotta do first | Close windows and doors, cover mirrors, turn off fans | Open a window so it gets fresh air | Turn on the fan to help it fly | Nothing, birds know how to stay safe indoors, they're clever
test: just held the bearded dragon, can i grab a snack now | Wash your hands with soap first | Yes, reptiles carry no germs at all | Yes, just wipe your hands on your top | Only wash your hands if it bit you
test: lizard ran off so i grabbed its tail, oops it came off?? | Many lizards drop their tail, never hold it there | That means you killed the lizard | Tails come off only on lizards that are already very sick | Glue it back on and it will heal
test: we going camping 4 days, the cat'll be fine with a big bowl of food right | No, ask a trusted person or pet sitter to look after her | Yes, cats can look after themselves | Yes, if you leave lots of water too | Yes, if you leave the window open
test: can i kiss the lizard, he's so cute | No, reptiles carry germs, wash your hands after | Yes, lizards are very clean, cleaner than dogs | Yes, if it is a baby | Yes, but only on the head
test: want a parrot, how hard can it be | Very hard, they live for decades and need lots of care | Easy, they just sit there | Easy, they eat once a week | Easy, they never make noise
test: Max says pet shop animals never carry germs. right? | No, any animal can carry germs, so wash your hands | Yes, shops clean them | Yes, only wild animals have germs | Yes, unless they look ill
test: heard someone's budgie died cos their mum burnt a non-stick pan in the kitchen, that's mad | It is real; fumes from overheated non-stick pans can kill birds quickly | It must be made up, pans cannot harm birds | It proves birds die from any cooking smell at all, even toast or soup | It means birds are only safe outdoors
test: my cousin picked up a lizard by its tail and it fell off, he says that's normal so it's fine | It is a defence, not fine; never hold a lizard by its tail | He is right, tails grow back so it is fine, no harm done | He is right, lizards like it | He is right, if you are gentle
test: someone says Kai's tortoise died cos his family is stupid, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is cruel and you do not know what happened | Pass it on, because tortoises live forever | Pass it on, so they learn | Pass it on, and add that it starved because nobody ever remembered to feed it
exam: Which of the following foods are recognised as poisonous to pet birds? | Avocado and chocolate | Apple and carrot slices | Seed and millet sprays | Lettuce and cucumber slices
exam: Identify the measures to be taken before permitting a bird to fly indoors. | The windows and doors closed, the mirrors covered and the fans switched off | The windows are opened for fresh air | The lights are switched off completely | The cage is removed from the room entirely so that the bird cannot return to it too soon
exam: It being given that a reptile has been handled, select the practice consistent with established guidance. | The hands are washed with soap, reptiles often carrying salmonella | The hands require no washing whatsoever if the reptile appears clean and healthy | The reptile is washed rather than the hands | The hands are wiped upon the clothing
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning reptiles and warmth. | Reptiles cannot produce their own heat and become ill if kept too cold | Reptiles produce abundant heat of their own and therefore prefer to be kept cold | Reptiles are unaffected by temperature | Reptiles must be kept in a refrigerator
